1
00:00:00,031 --> 00:00:04,395
Imagine you're moving an entire library every time someone wants to read a single book.

2
00:00:05,036 --> 00:00:11,202
You pack all the books into a truck, drive them to a new building, unload them, and repeat the same process again and again.

3
00:00:11,822 --> 00:00:13,604
Sounds ridiculously inefficient, right?

4
00:00:14,645 --> 00:00:17,828
That's exactly what traditional packet processing does.

5
00:00:17,848 --> 00:00:27,237
In traditional networking, when a packet travels from the hardware through the operating system kernel and up to an application, the actual bytes are copied across system boundaries.

6
00:00:27,237 --> 00:00:31,364
The network interface card, or NIC, receives the packet into one buffer.

7
00:00:31,865 --> 00:00:34,770
Then those bytes are copied into another buffer for processing.

8
00:00:35,552 --> 00:00:37,455
They may be copied again before transmission.

9
00:00:38,096 --> 00:00:42,424
Every copy consumes CPU cycles, memory bandwidth, and cache.

10
00:00:42,424 --> 00:00:45,528
High performance networking takes a completely different approach.

11
00:00:46,268 --> 00:00:49,332
Instead of copying the packet, it simply passes a reference to it.

12
00:00:49,772 --> 00:00:51,474
This technique is called zero copy.

13
00:00:51,935 --> 00:00:53,597
But what exactly is being passed?

14
00:00:54,117 --> 00:00:56,220
The answer is an mBuff.

15
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:59,624
Think of an mBuff as a small metadata structure attached to the packet.

16
00:01:00,284 --> 00:01:07,773
It contains information like the packet length, protocol details, and most importantly, the exact memory address where the packet's payload begins.

17
00:01:08,393 --> 00:01:10,656
Now let's look at where those bytes live.

18
00:01:10,656 --> 00:01:16,405
Before any packet even arrives, DPDK allocates a large collection of fixed-size packet buffers.

19
00:01:17,146 --> 00:01:18,829
This collection is called a mempool.

20
00:01:19,490 --> 00:01:25,219
You can think of it as a warehouse filled with thousands of empty packet containers all ready to be used.

21
00:01:25,239 --> 00:01:28,304
When the NIC receives a packet, it doesn't allocate new memory.

22
00:01:28,764 --> 00:01:38,179
Instead, the NIC uses Direct Memory Access or DMA to stream the incoming bytes straight from the wire directly into a free buffer in the mempool.

23
00:01:38,179 --> 00:01:43,128
no CPU involvement, no dynamic memory allocation, no unnecessary copies.

24
00:01:43,950 --> 00:01:48,719
From this moment onward, every stage of packet processing works with the same packet buffer.

25
00:01:49,501 --> 00:01:51,324
The passer receives the mBuff.

26
00:01:51,344 --> 00:01:53,248
The classifier receives the same mBuff.

27
00:01:53,869 --> 00:01:55,813
The firewall receives the same mBuff.

28
00:01:56,394 --> 00:01:59,239
The routing logic receives the same mBuff.

29
00:01:59,239 --> 00:02:03,708
Each component only receives a handle that points to the original packet buffer.

30
00:02:04,229 --> 00:02:06,613
The packet buffer never moves.

31
00:02:06,633 --> 00:02:12,424
Even if a router needs to modify a header or change an address, it modifies the bytes right there in place.

32
00:02:13,006 --> 00:02:16,873
They're still sitting exactly where the NIC originally placed them.

33
00:02:16,873 --> 00:02:19,257
This is the key idea behind zero copy.

34
00:02:19,918 --> 00:02:21,580
The packet isn't travelling through the system.

35
00:02:22,081 --> 00:02:23,103
Only the pointer is.

36
00:02:23,703 --> 00:02:26,027
Passing a pointer is incredibly cheap.

37
00:02:26,047 --> 00:02:35,942
It's typically just an 8-byte memory address on a 64-bit system compared to copying hundreds or even thousands of bytes across system boundaries for every packet.

38
00:02:35,942 --> 00:02:38,786
Now imagine processing millions of packets every second.

39
00:02:39,527 --> 00:02:47,337
If every packet required multiple memory copies, the CPU would spend most of its time moving bytes instead of making forwarding decisions.

40
00:02:48,058 --> 00:03:03,938
By eliminating those copies, zero copy dramatically reduces CPU overhead, lowers memory bandwidth usage, improves cache efficiency, and enables applications like DPDK to achieve tens or even hundreds of gigabits per second on modern hardware.

41
00:03:03,938 --> 00:03:10,370
Finally, when packet processing is complete, the NIC transmits the packet using the exact same memory buffer.

42
00:03:11,151 --> 00:03:16,882
After transmission, that buffer is returned to the mempool, ready to be reused for the next incoming packet.

43
00:03:17,423 --> 00:03:19,687
The packet buffer was allocated only once.

44
00:03:20,148 --> 00:03:21,129
It was never copied.

45
00:03:21,690 --> 00:03:27,040
Only the mBuff, the handle to that data, was passed from one component to another.

46
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:34,801
That's why, in high-performance networking, a packet is often best thought of not as a collection of bytes, but as a pointer to those bytes.

47
00:03:35,806 --> 00:03:37,857
And that's the essence of zero copy.
